  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 226 AM EST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nAnother pleasant and mild day today despite a weak, washed out, \nfrontal passage as another shortwave bypasses well north through the \nnorthern and eastern Great Lakes. This flattened, high latitude, \nflow then gives way to a full-latitude longwave trough over the \nEastern CONUS by the second half of this week. This transition will \ndrive a compact shortwave southeast through the area Wednesday \nafternoon-evening. The associated frontal wave and pacific moisture \norigins will bring chances (50-80%) for a period of rain during this \ntime, with a few heavier convective elements possible south of US-30 \nnear the sfc low track and steeper mid level lapse rates. \n\nColder air wraps in behind this low Thursday into Friday as several \nmore shortwave impulses drop through the region in deep northerly \nflow. A secondary trough and steepening lapse rates with the cold \npool will keep chances for scattered rain showers in the forecast \narea-wide into Thursday. This chilly northerly flow (850 mb temps \nfall to near -5C) brings about a full transition to lake effect rain \notherwise Thursday through Friday night. Favorable long lake \nfetch/convergence and thermal parameters will allow for more \norganized lake effect banding to settle into our northerly flow lake \nbelts at times, mainly west of US-31 in far nw IN (especially \nLaPorte/Starke counties Thursday night-Friday). Wet-bulb zero \nheights may collapse to less than 1 kft later Thursday night into \nFriday morning just inland from the lake. The NBM and ensemble \nguidance hint at the potential for slushy snow accums here (mainly \nLaPorte/Starke Ct). Confidence is low given lingering uncertainties \nregarding low level flow trajectories, warm lake and marginally cold \nnature to the incoming air mass.",
